prompt: Load the `kdevkit` skill from ~/.claude/skills/kdevkit/SKILL.md or ~/.kiro/steering/skills/kdevkit/SKILL.md — whichever path exists. Begin your response with the literal line `[kdevkit] applies` on its own line. Then answer in 4–8 sentences: I'm entering a feature in this kdevkit-aware project. Describe the entry decision and what happens before the first implementation slice, covering both modes — start a brand-new feature, and continue / pick up an existing one.
expect_substr: [kdevkit] applies
expected_narrative: A correct answer covers five behaviors. (1) **Entry mode resolution.** "continue / pick up <feature>" → look for the feature spec under specs/feature/ first; fall back to specs/backlog/ and promote (the literal verb "git mv" is not required — "promote" or equivalent is enough). "start <feature>" → if neither file exists, run the four short interviews. (2) **Spec-already-drafted rule.** When the feature spec on disk is populated, start in the planning phase — not the dev loop. A populated spec is not a *reviewed* spec; the agent confirms readiness with the user or iterates before any code work. (3) **Worktree-vs-branch decision** at session-start, driven by `kdevkit.prefer_worktree` in project.md (suggest a worktree on prefer; branch-only when silent — don't auto-run). (4) **Four interviews when starting fresh, in order: Requirements → Test Strategy → Design → Implementation Plan.** Tests sit immediately after requirements (not after design) so success criteria are declared before the design converges. The Test Strategy interview leans on project.md's Testing section to map onto existing layers. (5) **Phase gating** — stop after each interview phase and at each branch-phase boundary; the planning → dev cue ("spec looks good" / "start build" / "plan approved") is the gate before any code work. Wrong answers: jumping straight to coding when a populated spec is found; running interviews in the old Requirements → Design → Test Strategy → Implementation Plan order; auto-creating a worktree without checking project.md; running all four interviews without checking the backlog; chaining phases automatically.
tools: claude,kiro
